    {
      "id": "620480a4-53c8-493e-a65c-75794af23c17",
      "q_id": "SGD00183",
      "index": "SGD0001-3",
      "title": "As you probably know, coffee is the second largest commodity by traded value in the world, after oil. You probably also know that it's grown in Brazil, but you may not realize that it's not native to that country. It originated in East Africa. So if it came from there, how did it cross the Pacific? Well, the Dutch took coffee to a number of different places around the world, including, including South America, where it thrived. Nowadays, all the coffee cultivated in Brazil comes from that one seedling, which was of a variety called robusto. Now, because it all comes from the one plant, genetically, robusto isn't particularly varied, which unfortunately means its resistance to disease is, well, rather on the low side. And, well, this is exactly what has happened recently. A pathogen called coffee rust is ravaging coffee crops across South America. So what it looks like is that the price of coffee is, well, going to skyrocket on the wholesale markets unless production is increased dramatically in a short space of time in other countries, such as, you know, Vietnam, which seems rather unlikely. So, in short, there's a good chance that your morning coffee is going to become, well, quite pricey, and pretty soon at that.",
      "item": "SGD",
